Not exactly...it's Neutrality's answer to the Hand of Elbereth.

Lawful:  "You shall be the Hand of Elbereth!"
Neutral:  "You shall be my Envoy of Balance!"
Chaotic:  "Go forth and steal souls for Arioch!"

(or words to these effects).

> did some
>other neat stuff, and gave me the Vorpal Blade. I thought I saw it
>stated here just recently that a Valkyrie will always get Mjolnir
>first.

In almost all circumstances.  The exception is that if you're made the
Hand/Envoy/Soulstealer, you'll get your alignment weapon (Excalibur
for lawful, Vorpal Blade for neutral, and Stormbringer for Chaotic).
The weapon will automatically be blessed and rustproof (if you already
have the weapon, it will be made blessed and rustproof ["Your Vorpal
Blade goes snicker-snack!"  I don't know what the other messages are.]
You also get some nifty intrinsics (yay!) and a much longer prayer
timeout (boo!).  I believe I have had the experience of being given a
nifty mundane item by Crom just by praying (nowhere near an altar)
when I was the Envoy of Balance (it said an object appeared at my
feet).  That's never happened before, so I imagine it's a benifit of
being a Hand/Envoy/Soulstealer.  Anyone care to corroborate/contradict
this? 

Becoming the Hand/Envoy/Soulstealer requires very high alignment and
[I think] very good luck, so few people get it before getting their
first artifact from an altar in the normal way (which for Valks is
indeed Mjollnir).
 
>Second, why should I use it? Okay, it's a blessed rustproof longsword,
>presumably with nice bonuses. Big deal. I was already swinging a
>blessed rustproof +5 longsword (named DragonFang, thank you very
>much!), which seems to do a much quicker job of killing than the
>Vorpal Blade. I remember a short time ago, someone posted a list of
>the properties of magical weapons, but I don't have it any more.

It doesn't have great bonuses...just +1, I think (although you can
further enchant it).  Its major advantage is that it has a chance (1
in 20 is it?) of instantly decapitating and hence killing any monster
with a functional head you swing at.  This is slower than your +5
DragonFang for most monsters, but if you face a monster that's really
much too tough for your character (like the wumpus I met near a
polymorph trap in the Gnomish mines a couple of weeks ago), it can
give you a chance of pulling through...a 1 in 20 chance, in most
cases, but hey, that's better than your, say, 10th level character
stands against a minotaur if you're wielding a +5 normal longsword.

It's also nice against monsters (like the Wiz, for example) that like
to teleport away and heal.  Eventually, that technique will stop
working for them, as they'll just get their hes chopped off.

Keep trying for Mojo, though...in most situations it's the better
weapon.  If you're a Valk with 25 strength, it's DEFINITELY the better
weapon.
